Now that I can see the full script, a couple of important details stand out regarding how the plot is structured and what the code is attempting to calculate:

1. Progressive Vector Diagram (PVD) vs. Time-Series Quiver Plot
The Mismatch: Your comment states # Progressive vector plot using east and north velocity components, and you correctly calculate cumulative displacements (east_displacement and north_displacement). However, your quiver call passes the raw velocities (east_velocity, north_velocity) over time (x_dates on the X-axis, 0 on the Y-axis).

What this means:

If you want a True Progressive Vector Diagram (PVD), the X-axis should be East Displacement and the Y-axis should be North Displacement to show the actual geographic trajectory of a water parcel over time.

If you want a Current Vector Time Series / Stick Plot (velocities over time), you don't need east_displacement and north_displacement.

2. Date Limits Overwrite
You set vector_ax.set_xlim(deployment_start, deployment_end) near the top, but later down you call vector_ax.set_xlim(x_dates.min(), x_dates.max()), which overwrites your initial time bounds. If you want to force the plot to span your exact deployment window (even if there are slight data padding differences), you should keep deployment_start and deployment_end.

